            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall your house is relatively air tight, and there were just a few minor locations of air leakage noted during the blower door test:



** The front door of the house 



** The attic access panel



** Both bathroom fans could be upgraded to new Energy Star units that operate quieter, use less electricity &amp; have much less air leakage heat loss through them when not in use



Savings: With your house being very air tight already, it is tough to achieve a significant reduction. I did model a small 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to potentially save $ 41 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ. There are no rebates for air leakage reduction unless you participate in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, which requires the addition of at least R10 insulation to the main floor walls of your house. You had mentioned that you are not currently planning any significant renovations to your home.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and there appears to be another 1.5" (R7.5) of exterior semi-rigid insulation behind the current siding. As new housing in the NWT will normally have a minimum R30 in the walls, I thought I would model the estimated savings for adding another R11 to the exterior walls of your house. I know that you are not planning towards this upgrade, so this is just FYI.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R11, you could potentially save $ 153 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

Your current windows are older wood ones that appear to remain well sealed when closed. Two of the windows in the basement have already been updated and the one at the back is still an older one. As an FYI, I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the main floor windows (and the rear basement window) with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 307 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.9 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a combination boiler that provides both space heating (together with the Lifebreath Clean Air Furnace) and also on-demand domestic hot water. This is an older Baxi Luna 310 unit that I have seen elsewhere in Hay River and there have been some homeowners who have experienced problems with them. The domestic hot water usually stops working &amp; the homeowner will install a separate hot water heater. If you search this unit on the internet, you will find this is a common problem elsewhere. In your case, it is still working to supply both your heating &amp; hot water requirements.



The current Baxi Luna unit has a heating system performance of 83.8% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), while the hot water side performs at an efficiency of about 0.67 Energy Factor (EF). I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler with a minimum heating efficiency of 95% AFUE, and a hot water performance of 0.90 EF. The Lifebreath Clean Air Furnace does not need to be changed, although it should be looked at to see how much it operates. I think it is set to "continuous" operation which may be over-ventilating your house and wasting heat. You will know if the house is over-ventilated in winter if it is dry inside and there is never any condensation buildup along the bottom of your windows.



Savings: By replacing your current combination boiler with a new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler (at the efficiency levels noted above), you could potentially save $ 358 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. Just a note that this rebate is doubled to $1,500 for those particpating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (see the wall insulation upgrade recommendation for more about the DHERP).



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Pellet Stove Supplemental Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency combination boiler &amp; the cost of wood pellets purchased) if you were to use the wood pellet stove in the basement for about 50% of your basement heating requirements.



Savings: By using your wood pellet stove for 50% of the heating requirements of your basement, you could potentially save $ 168 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane combination boiler.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a Lifebreath Clean Air Furnace (incorporates Heat Recovery Ventilation)

        - the kitchen range hood fan

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - the clothes dryer



I think the Lifebreath is set to "continuous" operation which may be over-ventilating your house and wasting heat. You will know if the house is over-ventilated in winter if it is dry inside and there is never any condensation buildup along the bottom of your windows. Both bathroom fans seem to be operating at low efficiency &amp; you may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 9.6 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current propane combination boiler is direct vented and the pellet stove is power vented so less susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climate change!gh transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
